    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i checked the solenoids and they aren't throwing any specific error codes right now, but the pressure spikes are definitely there when it's under load. could it be something like debris or contamination in the fluid causing the valve body to stick up and down like that?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, debris or contaminated fluid trapped in the valve body is a very strong possibility for those erratic pressure spikes without solenoid error codes. Particulates can cause valves to stick or oscillate, leading to the irregular flow restrictions you are seeing under load. Additionally, a visually inspected dipstick will show if the fluid is burnt or thickened, but a pressure test with a temperature sensor is often needed to confirm internal mechanical damage. You may also want to inspect the transmission pan for metal shavings immediately to verify this contaminant theory before attempting any repairs.",
